.TH CONSOLE-FOLLOW 8 "2026-11-22" "gpu-switch" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
console-follow \- move the text consoles between the laptop screen and an external display on another GPU
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B console-follow
.RB [ auto | laptop | external | status ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Linux text console can show a console on one framebuffer only, and
there is one framebuffer per GPU: it cannot mirror across GPUs. On a hybrid
laptop whose external outputs are wired to the dGPU, the consoles (and so the
TTY login prompt) normally stay on the laptop screen.
.B console-follow
moves all consoles to another framebuffer.
.PP
It decides with these rules:
.TP
.I home
The GPU driving the laptop panel (an eDP, LVDS or DSI connector); without a
panel the firmware's primary GPU
.RI ( boot_vga ),
else the first framebuffer.
.TP
.I external
The first other GPU with a connected display. Without a laptop panel (a
desktop),
.B auto
only goes there when
.I home
has no display of its own.
.PP
A framebuffer shows on all displays of its GPU at once (mirrored). A single
display among several on the same GPU cannot be chosen.
.PP
The disk-unlock prompt and early boot messages always stay on the laptop
screen: they come before the dGPU driver is loaded.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B auto
Go
.I external
if there is one, else
.IR home .
Used by the optional udev rule.
.TP
.B laptop
Move all consoles
.IR home .
.TP
.B external
Move all consoles to the
.I external
GPU, or to another GPU even with no display connected.
.TP
.B status
List the framebuffers, their GPU and displays, mark the one with the
consoles (as root), and show what
.B auto
would choose.
.SH AUTOMATIC FOLLOWING
Optional. With
.BR "sudo ./install.sh \-\-with\-auto\-follow" ,
a udev rule runs
.B console-follow auto
when a framebuffer appears and on display hotplug events, so plugging or
unplugging a dock at the login prompt moves the consoles.
.B \-\-without\-auto\-follow
removes the rule and moves the consoles home. Without the rule nothing
moves by itself, and the commands above still work. When a framebuffer
disappears, the kernel moves its consoles away by itself.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
python3. The GPU drivers must provide framebuffers; for NVIDIA:
.B options nvidia_drm modeset=1 fbdev=1
in
.I /etc/modprobe.d/
(then reboot). Without it, there is no NVIDIA framebuffer and the consoles
stay on the laptop screen.
.SH INTERACTION WITH GPU-SWITCH
Consoles on the NVIDIA framebuffer keep
.B nvidia_drm
in use, so it cannot be unloaded. Before switching the dGPU off,
.B "gpu-switch apply"
always creates
.I /run/console-follow.hold
and runs
.BR "console-follow laptop" .
While the hold file exists,
.B auto
keeps the consoles
.IR home .
It is cleared at boot.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/82-console-follow.rules
The optional automatic following.
.TP
.I /run/console-follow.hold
Keep the consoles home.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Moves are logged to the journal, with the reason:
.BR "journalctl -t console-follow" .
.SH NOTES
Tested only on an ASUS GA401QM (AMD iGPU with the laptop panel, NVIDIA dGPU
with one external display). The rules for other setups (more GPUs, desktops)
follow from the description above but are untested.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gpu-switch (8),
.BR fbcon (4),
.BR udev (7)
